## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/fi.svg" width="32" alt="Finland" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Finland

Finland has undergone the most dramatic strategic realignment in its modern history since 2022, pivoting from decades of military non-alignment to NATO's most heavily militarized frontline member with the Alliance's longest Russian border. The country now fields one of Europe's largest reserve armies (280,000 wartime strength expanding toward 1 million) and spends 2.5% of GDP on defense with a target of 3% by 2029. Diplomatically, Finland demonstrated sophisticated engagement with China while firmly holding Western positions on Ukraine, and coordinated Alliance responses to territorial revisionism. The audit revealing significant gaps between claimed (10 billion) and actual (3.5-5 billion euros) government savings underscores severe fiscal pressures under the EU Excessive Deficit Procedure. Domestically, the right-wing Orpo government maintains stability while the leading opposition SDP faces a major escalating internal crisis over harassment allegations that could significantly affect 2027 electoral dynamics. Finland's institutional framework remains among the world's strongest, demonstrating sophisticated diplomatic engagement and continued NATO integration.

**Key developments:**

- **Economic:** State Audit Office concluded government's actual budget savings total 3.5 billion euros versus claimed 10 billion. Finance Ministry estimates 5+ billion effect by 2027. Major discrepancies include unfulfilled dynamic employment effects, incomplete welfare area savings, and tax cuts reducing savings impact.
- **Economic:** Finland's GDP grew 0.6% in Q4 2025 with employment and working hours increasing. Bank of Finland sees cautious labor market improvement signs. Multiple research institutes project 1.0-1.5% growth for 2026 driven by consumption and investment recovery.
- **Domestic:** SDP parliamentary group chair Tytti Tuppurainen faces allegations of inappropriate behavior toward staff and MPs from multiple sources. MP Pia Hiltunen publicly described inappropriate pressure over voting. SDP orders external investigation while party leader Lindtman defends Tuppurainen. Multiple parties launch similar internal investigations.
- **Domestic:** President Stubb and First Lady participated in Yle's 60-hour Muumi book reading marathon, drawing widespread public praise for animated performances and cultural engagement. Public comments emphasized uniqueness of having head of state reading to nation on television.
- **Diplomatic:** Orpo met Chinese President Xi Jinping and Premier Li Qiang, directly raising Ukraine concerns and calling on China to work toward lasting peace. Finnish companies signed 11 trade agreements worth significant value. Orpo stressed China's support for Russia undermines EU-China relations while maintaining commercial dialogue.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **PM Orpo's Beijing visit draws coalition partner criticism** — Prime Minister Petteri Orpo met Chinese President Xi Jinping and secured 11 trade agreements during visit to Beijing. Finns Party's Jani Mäkelä criticized meetings with Chinese leadership as 'problematic' while Orpo defended raising Ukraine concerns. *([yle.fi](https://yle.fi/a/74-20206666))*
- **President Stubb's Muumi reading marathon becomes cultural moment** — President Alexander Stubb and First Lady Suzanne Innes-Stubb participated in Yle's 60-hour Muumi book reading marathon, drawing widespread public praise for their animated performances and cultural engagement. *([is.fi](https://iltalehti.fi/tv-ja-leffat/a/276475a9-577b-45be-b012-eb7cb5455c20))*
- **Parliamentary harassment investigations spread across parties** — Following SDP scandal, multiple parties launch internal harassment investigations. Kokoomus completed study finding no systematic issues, Perussuomalaiset and others conducting similar reviews of workplace culture. *([iltalehti.fi](https://www.iltalehti.fi/politiikka/a/c347ead1-9e34-40bc-95e0-3f8f26f75501))*
- **Stubb and Norwegian PM warn against Greenland annexation** — President Alexander Stubb and Norwegian PM Jonas Gahr Støre published op-ed in Financial Times supporting Greenland's territorial integrity and warning against US annexation attempts amid Trump's statements. *([is.fi](https://ft.com/content/3dd30834-b120-43ec-91db-ffc064063465))*
- **Finance Minister Purra pushes welfare reform and fiscal consolidation** — Finance Minister Riikka Purra implemented income support cuts and proposed further welfare reforms, arguing current system doesn't incentivize work. Also addressed YEL pension reform and criticized opposition economic policies. *([suomenuutiset.fi](https://www.suomenuutiset.fi/toimeentulotuen-muutokset-voimaan-tanaan-purra-esittaa-myos-jatkouudistusta-harva-lahtee-yovuoroihin-siivoamaan-jos-saman-rahan-saa-tekematta-mitaan/))*
- **Bank of Finland's gold reserves reach historic high** — Finland's gold reserves are now worth 6.1 billion euros, the highest ever. Central bank maintains gold is stored across multiple countries including US, UK, Sweden and Switzerland as ultimate crisis insurance. *([iltalehti.fi](https://yle.fi/a/74-20207173?origin=rss))*
- **Orpo announces parliamentary working group on healthcare reform** — Prime Minister Orpo announces creation of parliamentary working group to address wellbeing services areas' future, responding to opposition pressure. Group to begin work in spring after PM's statement on healthcare services. *([hs.fi](https://hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000011787048.html))*
- **Social media restrictions for children gain political support** — Prime Minister Orpo, health authorities and two-thirds of Finns support banning or restricting social media use for under-15s, following Australia's model. Researcher describes current situation as 'uncontrolled human experiment'. *([yle.fi](https://yle.fi/a/74-20207494))*
